{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/figure-fields-2/reader-packet-1-part4.json'","aggregated_output":"{\n \"articles\": [\n  {\n   \"article\": 1,\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia Gains as Apple Pulls AI Off the Meter\",\n   \"publisher\": \"gurufocus.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-22\",\n   \"text\": \"Nvidia ( NASDAQ:NVDA ), the AI-accelerator and data-center platform leader, faced a fresh edge-computing test as Apple ( NASDAQ:AAPL ) pushed its new Macs as machines capable of running heavyweight AI privately instead of constantly calling the cloud. Nvidia shares gained approximately 0.7% to $229.08, suggesting investors were hardly treating Apple's move as an immediate threat to the GPU giant's core data-center engine.\\nWarning! GuruFocus has detected 3 Warning Sign with MSFT.\\nApple showed four Mac Studios working together to run a trillion-parameter model, using shared memory across the machines to tackle workloads once associated almost entirely with expensive server infrastructure. That is an eye-catching demonstration, but the scale gap remains enormous: Nvidia's latest quarter generated $89 billion of data-center revenue out of $96.2 billion overall, meaning roughly 92.5% of sales came from the infrastructure businesses powering large-scale AI. The GuruFocus picture reinforces that underlying strength, with Nvidia carrying a GF Score of 95/100 and particularly strong readings for profitability, growth and financial strength, while GF Value remains the noticeably weaker part of the profile.\\nThe bigger question is not whether a handful of Macs can run a giant model. They clearly can. The question is how much inference, coding and repetitive enterprise AI work permanently shifts from cloud infrastructure onto local machines. Apple could chip away at some private and predictable workloads, especially where data security and recurring token costs matter. But hyperscale training, massive networking demands and unpredictable peak workloads still play directly into Nvidia's territory. For Nvidia investors, the real risk starts only if local AI becomes a structural substitute for cloud inference rather than another layer of the computing stack.\",\n   \"named_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"figure\": 1,\n     \"text\": \"$89 billion of data-center revenue in the latest quarter\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"open_questions\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n     \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 2,\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia stock is approaching its cheapest valuation in a decade despite booming profits\",\n   \"publisher\": \"qz.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-22\",\n   \"text\": \"Nvidia stock is trading at less than 17 times forward earnings, half the multiple it commanded in 2025, even as revenue and net income are expected to surge\\nNvidia $NVDA stock is trading near its lowest valuation in more than a decade, according to Bloomberg , even as the chipmaker's revenue and net income are each expected to grow by roughly 90% or more in its current fiscal year.\\nAt under 17 times forward earnings, the stock's multiple has been cut in half compared with 2025 — a period when Nvidia 's expansion was actually slower — and has fallen sharply from above 25 times expected profits just this past May, according to Bloomberg.\\n\\\"The stock has de-rated pretty significantly, which suggests a healthy dose of skepticism that the company's current earnings power is sustainable,\\\" Eli Horton, a TCW senior portfolio manager overseeing thematic and durable growth equities, told Bloomberg. \\\"The stock's performance is surprising, given the backdrop of incredible fundamentals, but it tells you the market is expecting less than what the consensus is currently estimating.\\\"\\nOne drag on the stock's valuation is a squeeze on profitability. Nvidia 's gross margin came in at 75% last quarter but analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg show it sliding under 72% by the fourth quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter. Rising costs for key components such as memory chips are a central factor.\\nCompetition is another concern. David Russell, global head of market strategy at TradeStation, pointed to the growing threat of customers building their own silicon, noting that Meta $META Platforms and Alphabet $GOOGL are among the major Nvidia buyers that have moved to design AI chips internally — a shift he believes will gradually erode Nvidia 's pricing power and market share. \\\"Multiples expand when companies are well positioned with potential to get better, and Nvidia doesn't offer that,\\\" Russell said.\\nNvidia stock has gained 22% so far in 2026, putting it second among the Magnificent Seven behind Apple $AAPL 's 25% advance — yet that performance looks modest against the broader chip sector. The Philadelphia Stock Exchange Semiconductor Index has surged nearly 76%, with Intel $INTC and Advanced Micro Devices both more than tripling; Nvidia sits near the bottom of the index's leaderboard, even as the index itself carries a valuation of 20 times forward earnings.\\nNvidia Chief Executive Officer Jensen Huang has pushed back on the market's assessment, calling the company \\\"the world's first and only growth value stock\\\" and describing Nvidia as \\\"incredibly misunderstood\\\" at a Goldman Sachs $GS technology conference earlier this month.\\nThe valuation discount follows a stretch of strong results. Nvidia stock climbed roughly 7% after its second-quarter earnings , when revenue more than doubled and the company projected 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 — well above the 45% analysts had expected at the time.\\nHorton said the current multiple looks favorable given that neither a pullback in AI infrastructure spending nor a major regulatory intervention appears imminent. \\\"This seems like a very favorable multiple to have as an entry point,\\\" he said.\",\n   \"named_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"figure\": 1,\n     \"text\": \"the 45% revenue growth analysts had expected for fiscal 2028\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"figure\": 2,\n     \"text\": \"the 70% revenue growth projected for fiscal 2028\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"figure\": 3,\n     \"text\": \"gross margin sliding under 72% by the fourth quarter\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"open_questions\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n     \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 4,\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia Stock Could Surge by 2028: Here's How Beginners Can Get In Now According to Motley Fool's Price Estimates\",\n   \"publisher\": \"fool.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-23\",\n   \"text\": \"Even if you're a total novice to the stock market, you've probably heard of Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) .\\nNvidia is now the most valuable company in the world, worth more than $5 trillion. Its chips have formed the backbone of the AI revolution, and the top AI labs and cloud computing platforms rely on them to make AI applications run.\\nIf you were fortunate enough to buy Nvidia a decade ago and hold it, you would have made a huge return as the stock is up more than 14,000% during that time. That means a $1,000 investment in Nvidia would now be worth more than $14,000.\\nThe stock won't do that again over the next decade, but it still looks poised to soar by 2028. Here's how I see it.\\nThere's a lot of jargon in investing, but one of the most important concepts that beginning investors can learn is the price-to-earnings ratio , or P/E ratio. This is a company's price per share divided by its earnings per share.\\nNewbie investors often think that a stock's share price determines whether it is cheap or not. For instance, a $10 stock would be cheap and a $500 stock would be expensive, but the share price alone isn't really meaningful.\\nOwning a share of a stock is a claim on its earnings, so what really matters is how much earnings you get relative to the price of the stock. That's the P/E ratio. The lower it is, the cheaper the stock.\\nNvidia now trades at a P/E of 29, which is slightly higher than the S&P 500's P/E ratio of 25. The S&P 500 , an index that holds 500 of the biggest U.S. stocks, is generally seen as the market average. According to the numbers above, Nvidia is a bit more expensive than the S&P 500. However, the P/E ratio is backward-looking, and stocks are priced based on future earnings or cash flows.\\nBecause of its central position in the AI boom, Nvidia is growing much faster than the S&P 500. Wall Street expects the company to grow revenue by 91% this year and 66% next year. For fiscal 2028, which ends in January of that year, Nvidia is expected to have earnings per share of $15.68.\\nBased on that forecast, the stock trades at 14.6x next year's earnings. or a forward P/E of 14.6. If Nvidia maintains its current P/E ratio and hits that EPS forecast, the stock would double in less than a year and a half.\\nIf you're new to investing and you want to buy Nvidia stock , you'll first need a brokerage account if you don't already have one. You can open one up with a platform like Robinhood , Interactive Brokers , E*TRADE from Morgan Stanley , or Charles Schwab . There are a lot of options for brokers.\\nNvidia is currently trading around $228 a share, so you'll need that much to buy one share. However, many brokerages now offer fractional share purchasing , meaning you can buy a fraction of a share of Nvidia, allowing you to get ownership for less than the price of a share. Once you have an account, you'll need to search for the stock using the ticker, NVDA, and then choose an order type. Using a market order will execute the trade immediately and is the easiest way to buy a heavily traded stock like Nvidia. If you want to set a maximum price you're willing to pay, you can use a limit order.\\nKeep in mind that while Nvidia's future looks promising and the numbers favor continued gains in the stock, there is always risk in stock investing. There is no guarantee that the stock will go up between now and 2028. With any investment, it's a good idea to keep a long-term view and continue to follow the stock. Don't just buy and forget. If things change, you may want to sell.\\nStill, I think the odds are in your favor. As long as the AI boom continues over the next two years, Nvidia looks well-positioned to be a winner.\\nCharles Schwab is an advertising partner of Motley Fool Money. Jeremy Bowman has positions in Nvidia. The Motley Fool has positions in and recommends Interactive Brokers Group and Nvidia. The Motley Fool recommends Charles Schwab and recommends the following options: long January 2027 $43.75 calls on Interactive Brokers Group, short January 2027 $46.25 calls on Interactive Brokers Group, and short September 2026 $95 calls on Charles Schwab. The Motley Fool has a disclosure policy .\\nBy Jeremy Bowman – Sep 23, 2026 at 12:00AM EST\",\n   \"named_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"figure\": 1,\n     \"text\": \"earnings per share of $15.68 expected for fiscal 2028\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"open_questions\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n     \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n     \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n     \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?\",\n     \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 7,\n   \"title\": \"NVIDIA Stock Looks Expensive Until You Price The Vera Rubin Ramp\",\n   \"publisher\": \"trefis.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-24\",\n   \"text\": \"NVIDIA Stock Looks Expensive Until You Price The Vera Rubin Ramp\\nNVIDIA (NVDA) stock trades near $225, about 28.3 times its adjusted earnings over the past twelve months. Adjusted here means normalized net income with stock-based compensation added back, the same broad basis analysts use in their forecasts. For a stock near its 52-week high, that looks steep. But the multiple leaves out the growth analysts already expect, and that growth depends on how much NVIDIA can supply.\\nNVIDIA Looks Ordinary Once The Forecast Earnings Arrive\\nOn the earnings analysts expect for fiscal 2027, the same price is about 24.1 times. On what they expect for fiscal 2028, it is about 14.5 times. The price is the same. The earnings analysts expect it to buy are not.\\nNVIDIA expects Vera Rubin, its newest platform, to be the fastest product ramp in the company’s history. Management puts NVIDIA’s revenue opportunity at about $40 billion per gigawatt of capacity with Vera Rubin, against $25 billion with Blackwell. Production shipments began in August, with purchase orders already in from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud, and system OEM.\\nSo What Stands Between NVIDIA And Those Earnings?\\nFor fiscal 2028, management has guided about 70% revenue growth, the first time it has guided a full year ahead. That guide does not depend on finding more demand. The CEO says demand is much greater than 70%, and that supply is what holds the guide there. The CFO expects supply to stay a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme.\\nSome of that demand also leans on NVIDIA’s own balance sheet. NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs. Management concedes some will call this circular financing and says the compute can be redeployed to other customers.\\nAnalysts also disagree on what that growth earns. The 33 estimates of fiscal 2028 earnings run from $12.62 to $17.49 a share, a wide band for the year the forward multiple is priced on.\\nAnd NVIDIA’s Gross Margin Is Guided Lower As Memory Costs Climb\\nConsensus has earnings and revenue growing at a similar pace between fiscal 2027 and fiscal 2028, so analysts assume profit margins hold roughly steady. Gross margin is guided lower. Memory prices have risen more than management expected, and the guide has that margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, helped by price increases NVIDIA has already executed.\\nFor the forecast to hold, that lost gross margin has to be made up further down the income statement. That is the second condition behind the fiscal 2028 multiple, alongside supply.\\nSo Is NVIDIA Cheap, Or Only If The Chips Get Built?\\nOnly if the chips get built, which means the supply arrives and the margins hold. The trailing multiple cannot tell you either. How much would you stake on a year that has not started?\\nChecking that for every stock you own is a job most people have no time for. Since its inception, our rule-based Trefis High Quality Portfolio has outperformed its benchmark, a blend of three major indices.\\nOr hunt for these yourself: our Forward Valuation Discount screen ranks stocks priced furthest below their expected earnings. It shows where to look, and the forecast still has to come true.\",\n   \"named_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"figure\": 1,\n     \"text\": \"about 70% revenue growth guided for fiscal 2028\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"figure\": 2,\n     \"text\": \"the margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"open_questions\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n     \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n     \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n     \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?\",\n     \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 8,\n   \"title\": \"Credo vs. Nvidia: Which AI Chip Stock Is a Better Buy in 2026?\",\n   \"publisher\": \"fool.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-25\",\n   \"text\": \"The artificial intelligence boom has transformed the semiconductor landscape. Deciding between a high-growth connectivity specialist like Credo Technology Group ( CRDO +5.75% ) and the industry giant Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) requires a look at their specific niches.\\nBoth companies are central to modern data centers, but they solve different problems. Credo provides the high-speed wiring and connectivity chips that keep data moving, while Nvidia supplies the massive processing power AI models require. Comparing these two reveals how different players profit from the same infrastructure trend.\\nCredo designs high-speed copper and optical interconnect solutions essential for modern AI-driven infrastructure. The company operates as a niche provider of high-speed connectivity solutions among semiconductor stocks serving AI data centers. Credo relies on a limited number of customers for a substantial portion of its revenue, with sales to its top 10 customers accounting for approximately 90% of total revenue in fiscal 2026. This level of customer concentration adds risk to the business. The company also maintains a partnership with Oracle ( ORCL -0.13% ) to develop ZeroFlap optics to improve reliability in AI data centers.\\nFinancial performance has accelerated significantly recently. In fiscal 2026, which ended in May 2026, revenue reached nearly $1.3 billion, up roughly 205.7% from the previous year. This growth helped the company achieve a net income of approximately $472.3 million. The net margin for the period was approximately 35.4%, a substantial improvement from the 11.9% net margin reported in the prior fiscal year.\\nThe balance sheet remains highly liquid. As of its May 2026 balance sheet, the current ratio (a measure of short-term debt-paying ability) is nearly 10.2x. The debt-to-equity ratio (total debt compared to equity) is 0.0x, indicating no debt relative to equity. Free cash flow (cash from operations minus capital expenditures) was nearly $407.0 million. Stock-based compensation represented roughly 39.3% of operating cash flow, which inflates reported cash generation because SBC is a non-cash expense added back in the cash flow statement.\\nNvidia is the dominant provider of GPU-accelerated computing platforms used in gaming, professional visualization, and data centers. The company sells its products to massive cloud service providers and AI model makers globally. Nvidia relies on a concentrated customer base, with sales to two direct customers representing 22% and 14% of total revenue in fiscal 2026, respectively. This level of customer concentration adds a layer of risk to the business. The company also recently agreed to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.9 billion to expand its software capabilities.\\nRevenue growth remains a primary driver for the business. In fiscal 2026, which ended in January 2026, revenue reached nearly $215.9 billion, up roughly 65.5% year over year. This scale produced net income of approximately $120.1 billion. The net margin remained remarkably high at approximately 55.6%, consistent with the 55.8% net margin achieved in the previous fiscal year.\\nStrong profitability supports a robust capital structure. As of its January 2026 balance sheet, the current ratio is approximately 3.9x. The debt-to-equity ratio (which compares total debt to shareholder equity) is under 0.1x, meaning the company carries very little debt. Free cash flow (cash from operations minus capital expenditures) reached nearly $96.7 billion for the fiscal year. This massive cash generation provides significant flexibility for research, development, and strategic acquisitions.\\nCredo faces intense competition from larger, well-established semiconductor companies and potential new entrants in the data infrastructure market. The company depends heavily on a small number of customers, making it vulnerable to losses or shifts in purchasing patterns. Its business model relies on third-party manufacturing partners, exposing the company to supply chain disruptions and geopolitical risks regarding operations in Taiwan and mainland China. Furthermore, evolving international trade controls and export restrictions could restrict its ability to serve key markets.\\nNvidia faces significant risks from global geopolitical tensions and export control regulations, particularly for high-performance AI chips. These restrictions have impacted sales to China and could continue to limit market access. The company faces intense competition from other hardware suppliers and internal development efforts by customers like Microsoft ( MSFT +1.22% ) , Amazon ( AMZN +0.51% ) , and Alphabet ( GOOGL -0.61% ) ( GOOG -0.77% ) . Nvidia also manages various legal challenges, including inquiries from competition regulators worldwide regarding its market practices.\\nNvidia trades at a lower forward P/E based on future earnings estimates, while both carry a high P/S ratio compared to historical averages.\\nI'd buy Nvidia, and it isn't close. Here's why:\\nCredo's growth is not the issue. Revenue in its latest quarter more than doubled from a year earlier, and management guided the current quarter higher still. The stock fell anyway because gross margin guidance came in flat, and shares now sit well below their 52-week high. That's the problem with a company that gets about 90% of its revenue from 10 customers. Any hint of slower spending from one of them moves the stock, and the valuation leaves no room for a miss.\\nNvidia is growing nearly as fast at a scale that makes Credo look like a rounding error. Revenue in its latest quarter also more than doubled, with almost all of it coming from data center customers, and management's outlook for the current quarter assumes no data center compute sales to China at all. Nvidia has its own concentration risk, with two customers making up more than a third of sales, and the Hugging Face deal shows it's willing to spend to stay ahead.\\nFor a long-term investor, Nvidia offers the same AI infrastructure exposure at a lower price per dollar of expected earnings, with far less dependence on any single buyer. Credo belongs in a portfolio only as a small position you can afford to watch swing.\\nCredo more than tripled revenue last year but leans on 10 customers for 90% of sales, while Nvidia is doubling revenue at a scale nobody else in AI hardware can match.\\nBy Mike Schwenk – Updated Sep 25, 2026 at 3:00PM EST\\nCredo makes the high-speed connectivity chips and cables that move data between servers inside AI data centers, and revenue more than tripled in fiscal 2026.\\nNvidia's data center business brought in $89 billion in its latest quarter alone, and management guided the next quarter to $108 billion, excluding any China compute sales.\\nBoth companies depend on a small group of customers, but Nvidia's scale, margins, and lower valuation make it the stronger buy today.\",\n   \"named_figures\": [],\n   \"open_questions\": null\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 10,\n   \"title\": \"When Should You Buy NVIDIA Stock After This Run?\",\n   \"publisher\": \"trefis.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-28\",\n   \"text\": \"When Should You Buy NVIDIA Stock After This Run?\\nA $10,000 holding in NVIDIA (NVDA) stock three months ago was worth about $11,700 as of September 25, 2026. Yet against profits, the shares still trade near the low end of their ten-year range. If you have been waiting to buy NVIDIA, have you already missed it?\\nHave You Missed The Low Multiple On NVIDIA Stock?\\nNot if you judge it by earnings against its own past. The price-to-earnings ratio, or P/E, is the share price divided by a year of profit per share. NVIDIA trades at 28.1 times earnings , above the 22.1 for the S&P 500. Over the past ten years its P/E has ranged from 19.6 to 143.1, and today it is near the bottom.\\nBut a low P/E only stays low if profit holds up. NVIDIA’s operating margin, the share of revenue left after running costs, is 65%, its highest in ten years. Its three-year average is 59%. If margins slipped back toward that average, the same price would buy less profit.\\nCash looks less comfortable than earnings. Against operating cash flow, the cash the business brings in from running itself, the stock costs 40.4 times. The S&P 500 costs 14.5 times. Part of the cash has gone into inventory. On the fiscal Q2 2027 call, management said inventory rose to $32 billion as NVIDIA prepared for the Vera Rubin launch. On earnings or on cash, a price built on peak margins needs profit growth to continue.\\nHow Much Revenue Is NVIDIA Stock Counting On?\\nThe price appears to assume more revenue than NVIDIA itself has forecast for fiscal Q3 2027. Management guided revenue for that quarter to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. The consensus forecast for that quarter is $111.3 billion, above the top of that range.\\nThat expectation has a record behind it. Management said fiscal Q2 2027 revenue of $96 billion more than doubled from a year earlier. Growth also sped up for the fourth quarter in a row.\\nThe newly launched Vera Rubin platform is one source of that revenue. Management expects it to make up about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027. Management says supply, not demand, is the limit. Management expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028.\\nGross margin, the share of revenue left after making the product, is a different measure from operating margin. For fiscal Q3 2027 it is guided to 74%, plus or minus half a point, down from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027. Management pointed to higher component costs, especially memory. The next report has to beat NVIDIA’s own revenue forecast. The stock has not always taken its reports well.\\nNVIDIA’s Fiscal Q3 2027 Results Land in November\\nNVIDIA is expected to report on or around November 17, 2026. That report covers fiscal Q3 2027, and it will show whether revenue matches what the price appears to assume.\\nThe stock’s record after reports runs both ways. Over the last six reports, NVIDIA shares fell after four and rose after two, measured over two trading days. The biggest drop was 9.4%, after the February 25, 2026, report. The biggest gain was 3.8%, after the August 26, 2026, report.\\nA broad market sell-off is a separate risk. During the 2025 tariff shock, NVIDIA stock fell 32% from peak to low, against 19% for the S&P 500.\\nIf fiscal Q3 2027 revenue lands below $111.3 billion, the price will have asked for more than NVIDIA delivered. Buying now would then prove early. Suppose revenue clears $111.3 billion and gross margin comes in at 74% or better. The business will then have kept pace with the run. Buying at today’s price would not have been too late.\",\n   \"named_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"figure\": 1,\n     \"text\": \"the $111.3 billion consensus forecast for the quarter\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"figure\": 2,\n     \"text\": \"the $108 billion revenue guided for the quarter\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"open_questions\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n     \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n     \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n     \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?\",\n     \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n     \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA complete its approximately $12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face, agreed in 2026, by the check date?\",\n     \"period\": \"Check date 24 March 2028 (18 months after the 25 September 2026 item) — set per section 3 for a sized acquisition not yet completed, because the item gives no signing or closing date; also observable in NVIDIA's quarterly filings before then.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n     \"question\": \"Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology formally approve purchases of Nvidia's new RTX Pro 5500 high-end GPU by Alibaba, ByteDance and other Chinese firms by the end of Nvidia's fiscal 2027, allowing those sales to proceed?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3, because the item gives no date of its own.\"\n    }\n   ]\n  }\n ],\n \"answers\": [\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B31\",\n   \"article\": 4,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n     \"summary\": \"The Motley Fool article reports Wall Street consensus expectations that Nvidia's revenue will grow 91% in 'this year' and 66% in 'next year', and that Nvidia will earn $15.68 of earnings per share in fiscal 2028, which the article says ends in January of that year.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Because of its central position in the AI boom, Nvidia is growing much faster than the S&P 500. Wall Street expects the company to grow revenue by 91% this year and 66% next year. For fiscal 2028, which ends in January of that year, Nvidia is expected to have earnings per share of $15.68.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Wall Street consensus analysts (unnamed)\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"this year (fiscal year ending January 2027)\",\n       \"value\": \"91%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"Wall Street consensus expectation, year-over-year growth rate\",\n       \"party\": \"Wall Street consensus analysts (unnamed)\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"next year (fiscal 2028, ending January 2028)\",\n       \"value\": \"66%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"Wall Street consensus expectation, year-over-year growth rate\",\n       \"party\": \"Wall Street consensus analysts (unnamed)\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"earnings per share\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028 (ends January 2028)\",\n       \"value\": \"$15.68\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"Wall Street consensus expectation; GAAP/non-GAAP basis not stated\",\n       \"party\": \"Wall Street consensus analysts (unnamed)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B32\",\n   \"article\": 7,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management said it expects Vera Rubin, its newest platform, to be the fastest product ramp in the company's history, and put NVIDIA's revenue opportunity at about $40 billion per gigawatt of capacity with Vera Rubin, against $25 billion with Blackwell.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA expects Vera Rubin, its newest platform, to be the fastest product ramp in the company’s history. Management puts NVIDIA’s revenue opportunity at about $40 billion per gigawatt of capacity with Vera Rubin, against $25 billion with Blackwell.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA management\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue opportunity per gigawatt of capacity with Vera Rubin\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"about $40 billion per gigawatt of capacity\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"revenue opportunity, Vera Rubin\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA management\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue opportunity per gigawatt of capacity with Blackwell\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$25 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"as stated, per gigawatt of capacity with Blackwell\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA management\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-4\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Management's per-gigawatt revenue opportunity for Vera Rubin (~$40B vs ~$25B for Blackwell) and its expectation of the fastest ramp in company history give the unit economics and expected scale of the Vera Rubin ramp tracked in clean-4; they carry no shipment date and do not show that customer shipments have occurred.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e3\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management guided about 70% revenue growth for fiscal 2028, the first time it has guided a full year ahead, and said the guide does not depend on finding more demand; the CEO said demand is much greater than 70% and that supply is what holds the guide there.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"For fiscal 2028, management has guided about 70% revenue growth, the first time it has guided a full year ahead. That guide does not depend on finding more demand. The CEO says demand is much greater than 70%, and that supply is what holds the guide there.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA management (CEO)\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"about 70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"full-year guidance, first time the company has guided a full year ahead\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA management\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-4\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"A ~70% revenue growth guide for fiscal 2028 is management's forecast for the year the Vera Rubin ramp scales and so bears on the ramp's expected magnitude; it is a later-year revenue forecast and does not date FY2027 shipments or establish volumes.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e5\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided gross margin lower, with memory prices having risen more than management expected; the guide has that margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, helped by price increases NVIDIA has already executed. The article notes consensus assumes margins hold roughly steady.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Consensus has earnings and revenue growing at a similar pace between fiscal 2027 and fiscal 2028, so analysts assume profit margins hold roughly steady. Gross margin is guided lower. Memory prices have risen more than management expected, and the guide has that margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, helped by price increases NVIDIA has already executed.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA management\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"72% to 73%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"guided range, after memory-cost increases, helped by price increases already executed\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA management\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e6\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs; management concedes some will call this circular financing and says the compute can be redeployed to other customers.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Some of that demand also leans on NVIDIA’s own balance sheet. NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs. Management concedes some will call this circular financing and says the compute can be redeployed to other customers.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"investment in frontier AI labs\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"nearly $50 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"The nearly $50 billion NVIDIA has invested in frontier AI labs is the equity-investment side of clean-1's comparison against guarantees and credit support; management's answer to 'circular financing' criticism is its own statement, not a disclosure of guarantee amounts.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e7\",\n     \"summary\": \"The 33 analysts' estimates of NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 earnings range from $12.62 to $17.49 a share, which the article calls a wide band for the year the forward multiple is priced on.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Analysts also disagree on what that growth earns. The 33 estimates of fiscal 2028 earnings run from $12.62 to $17.49 a share, a wide band for the year the forward multiple is priced on.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Analysts (33 estimates compiled in the article)\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"earnings per share estimate range across 33 estimates\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"$12.62 to $17.49 a share\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"33 estimates\",\n       \"party\": \"analysts\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B33\",\n   \"article\": 8,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's fiscal 2026 (year ended January 2026) revenue reached nearly $215.9 billion, up roughly 65.5% year over year, producing net income of approximately $120.1 billion and a net margin of approximately 55.6%, consistent with the 55.8% net margin of the prior fiscal year, according to Fool.com's comparison article.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"In fiscal 2026, which ended in January 2026, revenue reached nearly $215.9 billion, up roughly 65.5% year over year. This scale produced net income of approximately $120.1 billion. The net margin remained remarkably high at approximately 55.6%, consistent with the 55.8% net margin achieved in the previous fiscal year.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2026 (ended January 2026)\",\n       \"value\": \"nearly $215.9 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"company total revenue, fiscal year\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\",\n       \"value\": \"roughly 65.5%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"company total revenue, fiscal year\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"net income\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\",\n       \"value\": \"approximately $120.1 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"company total, fiscal year\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 4,\n       \"measure\": \"net margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\",\n       \"value\": \"approximately 55.6%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"company total; prior fiscal year 55.8%\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's total revenue more than doubled year over year in its latest reported quarter, with almost all of it coming from data center customers, and its data center business generated $89 billion in that quarter alone, per Fool.com.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Revenue in its latest quarter also more than doubled, with almost all of it coming from data center customers, and management's outlook for the current quarter assumes no data center compute sales to China at all.\",\n      \"Nvidia's data center business brought in $89 billion in its latest quarter alone, and management guided the next quarter to $108 billion, excluding any China compute sales.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"data center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"latest reported quarter (as of 25 September 2026 article)\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"data center business\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e3\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management guided its next quarter to $108 billion of revenue, with an outlook that assumes no data center compute sales to China at all (excluding any China compute sales), as reported by Fool.com.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia's data center business brought in $89 billion in its latest quarter alone, and management guided the next quarter to $108 billion, excluding any China compute sales.\",\n      \"Revenue in its latest quarter also more than doubled, with almost all of it coming from data center customers, and management's outlook for the current quarter assumes no data center compute sales to China at all.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"next quarter (quarter following the latest reported quarter)\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"company outlook, excluding any China compute sales\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e4\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA recently agreed to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.9 billion to expand its software capabilities, as reported by Fool.com; the item does not say the transaction has closed.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"The company also recently agreed to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.9 billion to expand its software capabilities.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"recently\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"acquisition consideration for Hugging Face\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"approximately $12.9 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"agreed acquisition, not stated as completed\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e5\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA disclosed that sales to two direct customers represented 22% and 14% of total revenue in fiscal 2026, respectively, a customer concentration the article flags as a risk.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia relies on a concentrated customer base, with sales to two direct customers representing 22% and 14% of total revenue in fiscal 2026, respectively.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"sales to largest direct customer as share of total revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\",\n       \"value\": \"22%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"direct customer, company total revenue\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"sales to second direct customer as share of total revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\",\n       \"value\": \"14%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"direct customer, company total revenue\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ]\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B34\",\n   \"article\": 4,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"Wall Street consensus, as cited by Motley Fool's Jeremy Bowman, expects Nvidia to grow revenue by 91% this year and 66% next year (the article separately refers to fiscal 2028 as ending in January 2028, implying 'this year' is fiscal 2027 and 'next year' fiscal 2028).\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Wall Street expects the company to grow revenue by 91% this year and 66% next year.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Wall Street analysts (consensus)\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"this year\",\n       \"value\": \"91%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"Wall Street consensus expectations as reported by the article; year not explicitly labelled fiscal\",\n       \"party\": \"Wall Street analysts (consensus)\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"next year\",\n       \"value\": \"66%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"Wall Street consensus expectations as reported by the article; year not explicitly labelled fiscal\",\n       \"party\": \"Wall Street analysts (consensus)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"Consensus expectations, as reported by Motley Fool's Jeremy Bowman, put Nvidia's earnings per share for fiscal 2028 (the year ending January 2028) at $15.68.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"For fiscal 2028, which ends in January of that year, Nvidia is expected to have earnings per share of $15.68.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Wall Street analysts (consensus)\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Earnings per share\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028 (year ending January 2028)\",\n       \"value\": \"$15.68\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"Consensus expectation as reported by the article; GAAP/non-GAAP basis not stated\",\n       \"party\": \"Wall Street analysts (consensus)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B35\",\n   \"article\": 8,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"Nvidia disclosed that sales to two direct customers represented 22% and 14% of its total revenue in fiscal 2026 (the year ended January 2026), respectively, a level of customer concentration the article flags as a risk to the business.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia relies on a concentrated customer base, with sales to two direct customers representing 22% and 14% of total revenue in fiscal 2026, respectively. This level of customer concentration adds a layer of risk to the business.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"sales to a direct customer as a share of total revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\",\n       \"value\": \"22%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"direct customer, share of total revenue\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"sales to a direct customer as a share of total revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\",\n       \"value\": \"14%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"direct customer, share of total revenue\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"Nvidia recently agreed to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.9 billion to expand its software capabilities, according to the article, which does not describe the transaction as completed.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"The company also recently agreed to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.9 billion to expand its software capabilities.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"recently\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"acquisition consideration for Hugging Face\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"approximately $12.9 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"agreed acquisition, not stated as completed\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e3\",\n     \"summary\": \"Nvidia reported fiscal 2026 (year ended January 2026) revenue of nearly $215.9 billion, up roughly 65.5% year over year, net income of approximately $120.1 billion and a net margin of approximately 55.6%, versus a 55.8% net margin in the previous fiscal year.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Revenue growth remains a primary driver for the business. In fiscal 2026, which ended in January 2026, revenue reached nearly $215.9 billion, up roughly 65.5% year over year. This scale produced net income of approximately $120.1 billion. The net margin remained remarkably high at approximately 55.6%, consistent with the 55.8% net margin achieved in the previous fiscal year.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\",\n       \"value\": \"nearly $215.9 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"year-over-year revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\",\n       \"value\": \"roughly 65.5%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"net income\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\",\n       \"value\": \"approximately $120.1 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 4,\n       \"measure\": \"net margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\",\n       \"value\": \"approximately 55.6%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 5,\n       \"measure\": \"net margin\",\n       \"period\": \"previous fiscal year\",\n       \"value\": \"55.8%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e4\",\n     \"summary\": \"Nvidia's data center business brought in $89 billion in its latest quarter, and management guided the next quarter to $108 billion excluding any China compute sales; management's outlook for the current quarter assumes no data center compute sales to China at all.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia's data center business brought in $89 billion in its latest quarter alone, and management guided the next quarter to $108 billion, excluding any China compute sales.\",\n      \"Revenue in its latest quarter also more than doubled, with almost all of it coming from data center customers, and management's outlook for the current quarter assumes no data center compute sales to China at all.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"data center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"latest quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"data center business\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"next quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"excluding any China compute sales\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      }\n     ]\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B36\",\n   \"article\": 8,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's data center business generated $89 billion of revenue in its latest reported quarter, and total revenue in that quarter more than doubled year over year, with almost all of it coming from data center customers, according to the article.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia's data center business brought in $89 billion in its latest quarter alone\",\n      \"Revenue in its latest quarter also more than doubled, with almost all of it coming from data center customers\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"data center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"latest quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"data center business\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's management guided revenue for the next quarter to $108 billion, an outlook that assumes no data center compute sales to China at all, according to the article.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"management guided the next quarter to $108 billion, excluding any China compute sales\",\n      \"management's outlook for the current quarter assumes no data center compute sales to China at all\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"next quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"excluding any China compute sales\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e4\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA disclosed that sales to two direct customers represented 22% and 14% of its total revenue, respectively, in fiscal 2026.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia relies on a concentrated customer base, with sales to two direct customers representing 22% and 14% of total revenue in fiscal 2026, respectively.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"share of total revenue from one direct customer\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\",\n       \"value\": \"22%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"direct customer\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"share of total revenue from one direct customer\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\",\n       \"value\": \"14%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"direct customer\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      }\n     ]\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B37\",\n   \"article\": 1,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's latest reported quarter generated $89 billion of data-center revenue out of $96.2 billion in total revenue, meaning roughly 92.5% of sales came from the infrastructure businesses powering large-scale AI, according to GuruFocus's report on the company.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia's latest quarter generated $89 billion of data-center revenue out of $96.2 billion overall, meaning roughly 92.5% of sales came from the infrastructure businesses powering large-scale AI.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"data-center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"latest reported quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"data-center business\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"total revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"latest reported quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"company overall\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"share of sales from infrastructure businesses powering large-scale AI\",\n       \"period\": \"latest reported quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"roughly 92.5%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"derived by the article from data-center revenue versus total revenue\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B38\",\n   \"article\": 2,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"Nvidia's revenue and net income are each expected to grow by roughly 90% or more in its current fiscal year, according to expectations cited by Quartz alongside the company's valuation discussion. The item does not name the parties producing the estimate.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"even as the chipmaker's revenue and net income are each expected to grow by roughly 90% or more in its current fiscal year\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": null,\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"current fiscal year\",\n       \"value\": \"roughly 90% or more\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"expected; growth rate stated as roughly 90% or more\",\n       \"party\": null\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"net income growth\",\n       \"period\": \"current fiscal year\",\n       \"value\": \"roughly 90% or more\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"expected; growth rate stated as roughly 90% or more\",\n       \"party\": null\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"Nvidia's gross margin came in at 75% in the most recent quarter reported, Quartz said, citing the company's prior results.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia 's gross margin came in at 75% last quarter\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"last quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e3\",\n     \"summary\": \"Analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg show Nvidia's gross margin sliding under 72% by the fourth quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter; rising costs for key components such as memory chips are cited as a central factor.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg show it sliding under 72% by the fourth quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter\",\n      \"Rising costs for key components such as memory chips are a central factor.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"analysts compiled by Bloomberg\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fourth quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"under 72%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg\",\n       \"party\": \"analysts compiled by Bloomberg\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e4\",\n     \"summary\": \"Nvidia's revenue more than doubled in its second quarter, according to Quartz's account of the company's quarterly results.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"when revenue more than doubled\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"more than doubled\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e5\",\n     \"summary\": \"In its second-quarter earnings, Nvidia projected 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028, well above the 45% analysts had expected at the time, Quartz reported.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"the company projected 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 — well above the 45% analysts had expected at the time\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"company projection\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth expected by analysts\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"45%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"analysts had expected at the time\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B39\",\n   \"article\": 7,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management, as reported by Trefis, expects Vera Rubin, its newest platform, to be the fastest product ramp in the company's history, and puts NVIDIA's revenue opportunity at about $40 billion per gigawatt of capacity with Vera Rubin versus $25 billion per gigawatt with Blackwell.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA expects Vera Rubin, its newest platform, to be the fastest product ramp in the company’s history. Management puts NVIDIA’s revenue opportunity at about $40 billion per gigawatt of capacity with Vera Rubin, against $25 billion with Blackwell.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue opportunity per gigawatt of capacity with Vera Rubin\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"about $40 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"per gigawatt of capacity, Vera Rubin platform\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue opportunity per gigawatt of capacity with Blackwell\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$25 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"per gigawatt of capacity, Blackwell platform\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-4\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Management's per-gigawatt revenue opportunity for Vera Rubin ($40B) versus Blackwell ($25B) quantifies the value of the Vera Rubin ramp that clean-4 tracks; it is a management outlook, not a shipment confirmation.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev3\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management guided fiscal 2028 revenue growth of about 70%, the first time it has guided a full year ahead, as reported by Trefis.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"For fiscal 2028, management has guided about 70% revenue growth, the first time it has guided a full year ahead.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"about 70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"company-wide revenue growth; first full-year-ahead guidance\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev6\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs; management concedes some will call this circular financing and says the compute can be redeployed to other customers, as reported by Trefis.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Some of that demand also leans on NVIDIA’s own balance sheet. NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs. Management concedes some will call this circular financing and says the compute can be redeployed to other customers.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"investment in frontier AI labs\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"nearly $50 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"equity investment; cumulative\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Reports NVIDIA's roughly $50 billion of equity investment in frontier AI labs — the equity-investment side clean-1 compares against guarantees/credit support — and management's response to the 'circular financing' concern.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev7\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided its gross margin lower to settle in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, as memory prices have risen more than management expected, partly offset by price increases NVIDIA has already executed, as reported by Trefis.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Gross margin is guided lower. Memory prices have risen more than management expected, and the guide has that margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, helped by price increases NVIDIA has already executed.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"72% to 73%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"company gross margin; reflects higher memory costs partly offset by executed price increases\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev8\",\n     \"summary\": \"The 33 analyst estimates of NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 earnings per share range from $12.62 to $17.49 a share, a wide band for the year the forward multiple is priced on, according to Trefis.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Analysts also disagree on what that growth earns. The 33 estimates of fiscal 2028 earnings run from $12.62 to $17.49 a share, a wide band for the year the forward multiple is priced on.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Trefis\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"earnings per share\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"$12.62 to $17.49 a share\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"range across 33 analyst estimates\",\n       \"party\": \"Trefis\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B40\",\n   \"article\": 10,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n     \"summary\": \"On NVIDIA's fiscal Q2 2027 earnings call, management said inventory rose to $32 billion as the company prepared for the Vera Rubin launch.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Part of the cash has gone into inventory. On the fiscal Q2 2027 call, management said inventory rose to $32 billion as NVIDIA prepared for the Vera Rubin launch.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"inventory\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$32 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-4\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"A $32 billion inventory build made in preparation for the Vera Rubin launch is a supply-side signal on how far the ramp toward volume shipments of Vera Rubin has progressed; it does not itself confirm shipments or revenue recognition.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management guided fiscal Q3 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, while the consensus forecast for that quarter is $111.3 billion, above the top of that guidance range.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"The price appears to assume more revenue than NVIDIA itself has forecast for fiscal Q3 2027. Management guided revenue for that quarter to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. The consensus forecast for that quarter is $111.3 billion, above the top of that range.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA (guidance); consensus estimate as reported by Trefis\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"plus or minus 2%\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA (guidance); consensus estimate as reported by Trefis\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"consensus revenue forecast\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$111.3 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"consensus forecast, above the top of NVIDIA's guidance range\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA (guidance); consensus estimate as reported by Trefis\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management said fiscal Q2 2027 revenue was $96 billion, more than double the year-earlier figure, with growth accelerating for the fourth consecutive quarter.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"That expectation has a record behind it. Management said fiscal Q2 2027 revenue of $96 billion more than doubled from a year earlier. Growth also sped up for the fourth quarter in a row.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$96 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"more than doubled from a year earlier; growth accelerated for the fourth consecutive quarter\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-4\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided gross margin for fiscal Q3 2027 to 74%, plus or minus half a point, down from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027, with management pointing to higher component costs, especially memory.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Gross margin, the share of revenue left after making the product, is a different measure from operating margin. For fiscal Q3 2027 it is guided to 74%, plus or minus half a point, down from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027. Management pointed to higher component costs, especially memory.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"74%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"plus or minus half a point; attributed to higher component costs, especially memory\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-5\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management expects the newly launched Vera Rubin platform to make up about 20% of its data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"The newly launched Vera Rubin platform is one source of that revenue. Management expects it to make up about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Vera Rubin share of data center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"about 20%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"of data center revenue; management expectation\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-4\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Management's expectation that Vera Rubin will already contribute about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027 bears on whether the platform reaches volume customer shipments and first revenue recognition within fiscal 2027; it is a forecast, not confirmed shipment or revenue.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-7\",\n     \"summary\": \"Trefis reports that NVIDIA's operating margin is 65%, its highest in ten years, against a three-year average of 59%.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"But a low P/E only stays low if profit holds up. NVIDIA’s operating margin, the share of revenue left after running costs, is 65%, its highest in ten years. Its three-year average is 59%.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Trefis (analysis of NVIDIA reported figures)\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"operating margin\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"65%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"highest in ten years\",\n       \"party\": \"Trefis (analysis of NVIDIA reported figures)\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"operating margin, three-year average\",\n       \"period\": \"trailing three years\",\n       \"value\": \"59%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"three-year average\",\n       \"party\": \"Trefis (analysis of NVIDIA reported figures)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  }\n ]\n}","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/figure-fields-2/reader-packet-1-part4.json\\\")); print(json.dumps([{\\\"article\\\":a[\\\"article\\\"],\\\"title\\\":a[\\\"title\\\"],\\\"text\\\":a[\\\"text\\\"],\\\"named\\\":a.get(\\\"named_figures\\\",[]),\\\"questions\\\":a.get(\\\"open_questions\\\",[])} for a in p[\\\"articles\\\"] if a[\\\"article\\\"] not in [1,2,4,7]],ensure_ascii=False)); print(\\\"ANSWERS\\\"); print(json.dumps(p[\\\"answers\\\"],ensure_ascii=False))'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/figure-fields-2/reader-packet-1-part4.json\\\")); print(json.dumps([{\\\"article\\\":a[\\\"article\\\"],\\\"title\\\":a[\\\"title\\\"],\\\"text\\\":a[\\\"text\\\"],\\\"named\\\":a.get(\\\"named_figures\\\",[]),\\\"questions\\\":a.get(\\\"open_questions\\\",[])} for a in p[\\\"articles\\\"] if a[\\\"article\\\"] not in [1,2,4,7]],ensure_ascii=False)); print(\\\"ANSWERS\\\"); print(json.dumps(p[\\\"answers\\\"],ensure_ascii=False))'\"","aggregated_output":"[{\"article\": 8, \"title\": \"Credo vs. Nvidia: Which AI Chip Stock Is a Better Buy in 2026?\", \"text\": \"The artificial intelligence boom has transformed the semiconductor landscape. Deciding between a high-growth connectivity specialist like Credo Technology Group ( CRDO +5.75% ) and the industry giant Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) requires a look at their specific niches.\\nBoth companies are central to modern data centers, but they solve different problems. Credo provides the high-speed wiring and connectivity chips that keep data moving, while Nvidia supplies the massive processing power AI models require. Comparing these two reveals how different players profit from the same infrastructure trend.\\nCredo designs high-speed copper and optical interconnect solutions essential for modern AI-driven infrastructure. The company operates as a niche provider of high-speed connectivity solutions among semiconductor stocks serving AI data centers. Credo relies on a limited number of customers for a substantial portion of its revenue, with sales to its top 10 customers accounting for approximately 90% of total revenue in fiscal 2026. This level of customer concentration adds risk to the business. The company also maintains a partnership with Oracle ( ORCL -0.13% ) to develop ZeroFlap optics to improve reliability in AI data centers.\\nFinancial performance has accelerated significantly recently. In fiscal 2026, which ended in May 2026, revenue reached nearly $1.3 billion, up roughly 205.7% from the previous year. This growth helped the company achieve a net income of approximately $472.3 million. The net margin for the period was approximately 35.4%, a substantial improvement from the 11.9% net margin reported in the prior fiscal year.\\nThe balance sheet remains highly liquid. As of its May 2026 balance sheet, the current ratio (a measure of short-term debt-paying ability) is nearly 10.2x. The debt-to-equity ratio (total debt compared to equity) is 0.0x, indicating no debt relative to equity. Free cash flow (cash from operations minus capital expenditures) was nearly $407.0 million. Stock-based compensation represented roughly 39.3% of operating cash flow, which inflates reported cash generation because SBC is a non-cash expense added back in the cash flow statement.\\nNvidia is the dominant provider of GPU-accelerated computing platforms used in gaming, professional visualization, and data centers. The company sells its products to massive cloud service providers and AI model makers globally. Nvidia relies on a concentrated customer base, with sales to two direct customers representing 22% and 14% of total revenue in fiscal 2026, respectively. This level of customer concentration adds a layer of risk to the business. The company also recently agreed to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.9 billion to expand its software capabilities.\\nRevenue growth remains a primary driver for the business. In fiscal 2026, which ended in January 2026, revenue reached nearly $215.9 billion, up roughly 65.5% year over year. This scale produced net income of approximately $120.1 billion. The net margin remained remarkably high at approximately 55.6%, consistent with the 55.8% net margin achieved in the previous fiscal year.\\nStrong profitability supports a robust capital structure. As of its January 2026 balance sheet, the current ratio is approximately 3.9x. The debt-to-equity ratio (which compares total debt to shareholder equity) is under 0.1x, meaning the company carries very little debt. Free cash flow (cash from operations minus capital expenditures) reached nearly $96.7 billion for the fiscal year. This massive cash generation provides significant flexibility for research, development, and strategic acquisitions.\\nCredo faces intense competition from larger, well-established semiconductor companies and potential new entrants in the data infrastructure market. The company depends heavily on a small number of customers, making it vulnerable to losses or shifts in purchasing patterns. Its business model relies on third-party manufacturing partners, exposing the company to supply chain disruptions and geopolitical risks regarding operations in Taiwan and mainland China. Furthermore, evolving international trade controls and export restrictions could restrict its ability to serve key markets.\\nNvidia faces significant risks from global geopolitical tensions and export control regulations, particularly for high-performance AI chips. These restrictions have impacted sales to China and could continue to limit market access. The company faces intense competition from other hardware suppliers and internal development efforts by customers like Microsoft ( MSFT +1.22% ) , Amazon ( AMZN +0.51% ) , and Alphabet ( GOOGL -0.61% ) ( GOOG -0.77% ) . Nvidia also manages various legal challenges, including inquiries from competition regulators worldwide regarding its market practices.\\nNvidia trades at a lower forward P/E based on future earnings estimates, while both carry a high P/S ratio compared to historical averages.\\nI'd buy Nvidia, and it isn't close. Here's why:\\nCredo's growth is not the issue. Revenue in its latest quarter more than doubled from a year earlier, and management guided the current quarter higher still. The stock fell anyway because gross margin guidance came in flat, and shares now sit well below their 52-week high. That's the problem with a company that gets about 90% of its revenue from 10 customers. Any hint of slower spending from one of them moves the stock, and the valuation leaves no room for a miss.\\nNvidia is growing nearly as fast at a scale that makes Credo look like a rounding error. Revenue in its latest quarter also more than doubled, with almost all of it coming from data center customers, and management's outlook for the current quarter assumes no data center compute sales to China at all. Nvidia has its own concentration risk, with two customers making up more than a third of sales, and the Hugging Face deal shows it's willing to spend to stay ahead.\\nFor a long-term investor, Nvidia offers the same AI infrastructure exposure at a lower price per dollar of expected earnings, with far less dependence on any single buyer. Credo belongs in a portfolio only as a small position you can afford to watch swing.\\nCredo more than tripled revenue last year but leans on 10 customers for 90% of sales, while Nvidia is doubling revenue at a scale nobody else in AI hardware can match.\\nBy Mike Schwenk – Updated Sep 25, 2026 at 3:00PM EST\\nCredo makes the high-speed connectivity chips and cables that move data between servers inside AI data centers, and revenue more than tripled in fiscal 2026.\\nNvidia's data center business brought in $89 billion in its latest quarter alone, and management guided the next quarter to $108 billion, excluding any China compute sales.\\nBoth companies depend on a small group of customers, but Nvidia's scale, margins, and lower valuation make it the stronger buy today.\", \"named\": [], \"questions\": null}, {\"article\": 10, \"title\": \"When Should You Buy NVIDIA Stock After This Run?\", \"text\": \"When Should You Buy NVIDIA Stock After This Run?\\nA $10,000 holding in NVIDIA (NVDA) stock three months ago was worth about $11,700 as of September 25, 2026. Yet against profits, the shares still trade near the low end of their ten-year range. If you have been waiting to buy NVIDIA, have you already missed it?\\nHave You Missed The Low Multiple On NVIDIA Stock?\\nNot if you judge it by earnings against its own past. The price-to-earnings ratio, or P/E, is the share price divided by a year of profit per share. NVIDIA trades at 28.1 times earnings , above the 22.1 for the S&P 500. Over the past ten years its P/E has ranged from 19.6 to 143.1, and today it is near the bottom.\\nBut a low P/E only stays low if profit holds up. NVIDIA’s operating margin, the share of revenue left after running costs, is 65%, its highest in ten years. Its three-year average is 59%. If margins slipped back toward that average, the same price would buy less profit.\\nCash looks less comfortable than earnings. Against operating cash flow, the cash the business brings in from running itself, the stock costs 40.4 times. The S&P 500 costs 14.5 times. Part of the cash has gone into inventory. On the fiscal Q2 2027 call, management said inventory rose to $32 billion as NVIDIA prepared for the Vera Rubin launch. On earnings or on cash, a price built on peak margins needs profit growth to continue.\\nHow Much Revenue Is NVIDIA Stock Counting On?\\nThe price appears to assume more revenue than NVIDIA itself has forecast for fiscal Q3 2027. Management guided revenue for that quarter to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. The consensus forecast for that quarter is $111.3 billion, above the top of that range.\\nThat expectation has a record behind it. Management said fiscal Q2 2027 revenue of $96 billion more than doubled from a year earlier. Growth also sped up for the fourth quarter in a row.\\nThe newly launched Vera Rubin platform is one source of that revenue. Management expects it to make up about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027. Management says supply, not demand, is the limit. Management expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028.\\nGross margin, the share of revenue left after making the product, is a different measure from operating margin. For fiscal Q3 2027 it is guided to 74%, plus or minus half a point, down from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027. Management pointed to higher component costs, especially memory. The next report has to beat NVIDIA’s own revenue forecast. The stock has not always taken its reports well.\\nNVIDIA’s Fiscal Q3 2027 Results Land in November\\nNVIDIA is expected to report on or around November 17, 2026. That report covers fiscal Q3 2027, and it will show whether revenue matches what the price appears to assume.\\nThe stock’s record after reports runs both ways. Over the last six reports, NVIDIA shares fell after four and rose after two, measured over two trading days. The biggest drop was 9.4%, after the February 25, 2026, report. The biggest gain was 3.8%, after the August 26, 2026, report.\\nA broad market sell-off is a separate risk. During the 2025 tariff shock, NVIDIA stock fell 32% from peak to low, against 19% for the S&P 500.\\nIf fiscal Q3 2027 revenue lands below $111.3 billion, the price will have asked for more than NVIDIA delivered. Buying now would then prove early. Suppose revenue clears $111.3 billion and gross margin comes in at 74% or better. The business will then have kept pace with the run. Buying at today’s price would not have been too late.\", \"named\": [{\"figure\": 1, \"text\": \"the $111.3 billion consensus forecast for the quarter\"}, {\"figure\": 2, \"text\": \"the $108 billion revenue guided for the quarter\"}], \"questions\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\", \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\", \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\", \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\", \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?\", \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA complete its approximately $12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face, agreed in 2026, by the check date?\", \"period\": \"Check date 24 March 2028 (18 months after the 25 September 2026 item) — set per section 3 for a sized acquisition not yet completed, because the item gives no signing or closing date; also observable in NVIDIA's quarterly filings before then.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-7\", \"question\": \"Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology formally approve purchases of Nvidia's new RTX Pro 5500 high-end GPU by Alibaba, ByteDance and other Chinese firms by the end of Nvidia's fiscal 2027, allowing those sales to proceed?\", \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3, because the item gives no date of its own.\"}]}]\nANSWERS\n[{\"answer\": \"B31\", \"article\": 4, \"statements_with_figures\": [{\"id\": \"ev-1\", \"summary\": \"The Motley Fool article reports Wall Street consensus expectations that Nvidia's revenue will grow 91% in 'this year' and 66% in 'next year', and that Nvidia will earn $15.68 of earnings per share in fiscal 2028, which the article says ends in January of that year.\", \"passages\": [\"Because of its central position in the AI boom, Nvidia is growing much faster than the S&P 500. Wall Street expects the company to grow revenue by 91% this year and 66% next year. For fiscal 2028, which ends in January of that year, Nvidia is expected to have earnings per share of $15.68.\"], \"statement_party\": \"Wall Street consensus analysts (unnamed)\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"revenue growth\", \"period\": \"this year (fiscal year ending January 2027)\", \"value\": \"91%\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"Wall Street consensus expectation, year-over-year growth rate\", \"party\": \"Wall Street consensus analysts (unnamed)\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"revenue growth\", \"period\": \"next year (fiscal 2028, ending January 2028)\", \"value\": \"66%\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"Wall Street consensus expectation, year-over-year growth rate\", \"party\": \"Wall Street consensus analysts (unnamed)\"}, {\"n\": 3, \"measure\": \"earnings per share\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2028 (ends January 2028)\", \"value\": \"$15.68\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"Wall Street consensus expectation; GAAP/non-GAAP basis not stated\", \"party\": \"Wall Street consensus analysts (unnamed)\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}]}, {\"answer\": \"B32\", \"article\": 7, \"statements_with_figures\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management said it expects Vera Rubin, its newest platform, to be the fastest product ramp in the company's history, and put NVIDIA's revenue opportunity at about $40 billion per gigawatt of capacity with Vera Rubin, against $25 billion with Blackwell.\", \"passages\": [\"NVIDIA expects Vera Rubin, its newest platform, to be the fastest product ramp in the company’s history. Management puts NVIDIA’s revenue opportunity at about $40 billion per gigawatt of capacity with Vera Rubin, against $25 billion with Blackwell.\"], \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA management\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"revenue opportunity per gigawatt of capacity with Vera Rubin\", \"period\": null, \"value\": \"about $40 billion per gigawatt of capacity\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"revenue opportunity, Vera Rubin\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA management\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"revenue opportunity per gigawatt of capacity with Blackwell\", \"period\": null, \"value\": \"$25 billion\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"as stated, per gigawatt of capacity with Blackwell\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA management\"}], \"links\": [{\"question\": \"clean-4\", \"new_question\": false, \"reason\": \"Management's per-gigawatt revenue opportunity for Vera Rubin (~$40B vs ~$25B for Blackwell) and its expectation of the fastest ramp in company history give the unit economics and expected scale of the Vera Rubin ramp tracked in clean-4; they carry no shipment date and do not show that customer shipments have occurred.\"}]}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management guided about 70% revenue growth for fiscal 2028, the first time it has guided a full year ahead, and said the guide does not depend on finding more demand; the CEO said demand is much greater than 70% and that supply is what holds the guide there.\", \"passages\": [\"For fiscal 2028, management has guided about 70% revenue growth, the first time it has guided a full year ahead. That guide does not depend on finding more demand. The CEO says demand is much greater than 70%, and that supply is what holds the guide there.\"], \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA management (CEO)\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"revenue growth\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\", \"value\": \"about 70%\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"full-year guidance, first time the company has guided a full year ahead\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA management\"}], \"links\": [{\"question\": \"clean-4\", \"new_question\": false, \"reason\": \"A ~70% revenue growth guide for fiscal 2028 is management's forecast for the year the Vera Rubin ramp scales and so bears on the ramp's expected magnitude; it is a later-year revenue forecast and does not date FY2027 shipments or establish volumes.\"}]}, {\"id\": \"e5\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided gross margin lower, with memory prices having risen more than management expected; the guide has that margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, helped by price increases NVIDIA has already executed. The article notes consensus assumes margins hold roughly steady.\", \"passages\": [\"Consensus has earnings and revenue growing at a similar pace between fiscal 2027 and fiscal 2028, so analysts assume profit margins hold roughly steady. Gross margin is guided lower. Memory prices have risen more than management expected, and the guide has that margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, helped by price increases NVIDIA has already executed.\"], \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA management\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"gross margin\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\", \"value\": \"72% to 73%\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"guided range, after memory-cost increases, helped by price increases already executed\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA management\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}, {\"id\": \"e6\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs; management concedes some will call this circular financing and says the compute can be redeployed to other customers.\", \"passages\": [\"Some of that demand also leans on NVIDIA’s own balance sheet. NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs. Management concedes some will call this circular financing and says the compute can be redeployed to other customers.\"], \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"investment in frontier AI labs\", \"period\": null, \"value\": \"nearly $50 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": [{\"question\": \"clean-1\", \"new_question\": false, \"reason\": \"The nearly $50 billion NVIDIA has invested in frontier AI labs is the equity-investment side of clean-1's comparison against guarantees and credit support; management's answer to 'circular financing' criticism is its own statement, not a disclosure of guarantee amounts.\"}]}, {\"id\": \"e7\", \"summary\": \"The 33 analysts' estimates of NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 earnings range from $12.62 to $17.49 a share, which the article calls a wide band for the year the forward multiple is priced on.\", \"passages\": [\"Analysts also disagree on what that growth earns. The 33 estimates of fiscal 2028 earnings run from $12.62 to $17.49 a share, a wide band for the year the forward multiple is priced on.\"], \"statement_party\": \"Analysts (33 estimates compiled in the article)\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"earnings per share estimate range across 33 estimates\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\", \"value\": \"$12.62 to $17.49 a share\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"33 estimates\", \"party\": \"analysts\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}]}, {\"answer\": \"B33\", \"article\": 8, \"statements_with_figures\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's fiscal 2026 (year ended January 2026) revenue reached nearly $215.9 billion, up roughly 65.5% year over year, producing net income of approximately $120.1 billion and a net margin of approximately 55.6%, consistent with the 55.8% net margin of the prior fiscal year, according to Fool.com's comparison article.\", \"passages\": [\"In fiscal 2026, which ended in January 2026, revenue reached nearly $215.9 billion, up roughly 65.5% year over year. This scale produced net income of approximately $120.1 billion. The net margin remained remarkably high at approximately 55.6%, consistent with the 55.8% net margin achieved in the previous fiscal year.\"], \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2026 (ended January 2026)\", \"value\": \"nearly $215.9 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"company total revenue, fiscal year\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"revenue growth year over year\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\", \"value\": \"roughly 65.5%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"company total revenue, fiscal year\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 3, \"measure\": \"net income\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\", \"value\": \"approximately $120.1 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"company total, fiscal year\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 4, \"measure\": \"net margin\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\", \"value\": \"approximately 55.6%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"company total; prior fiscal year 55.8%\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}]}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's total revenue more than doubled year over year in its latest reported quarter, with almost all of it coming from data center customers, and its data center business generated $89 billion in that quarter alone, per Fool.com.\", \"passages\": [\"Revenue in its latest quarter also more than doubled, with almost all of it coming from data center customers, and management's outlook for the current quarter assumes no data center compute sales to China at all.\", \"Nvidia's data center business brought in $89 billion in its latest quarter alone, and management guided the next quarter to $108 billion, excluding any China compute sales.\"], \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"data center revenue\", \"period\": \"latest reported quarter (as of 25 September 2026 article)\", \"value\": \"$89 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"data center business\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}]}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management guided its next quarter to $108 billion of revenue, with an outlook that assumes no data center compute sales to China at all (excluding any China compute sales), as reported by Fool.com.\", \"passages\": [\"Nvidia's data center business brought in $89 billion in its latest quarter alone, and management guided the next quarter to $108 billion, excluding any China compute sales.\", \"Revenue in its latest quarter also more than doubled, with almost all of it coming from data center customers, and management's outlook for the current quarter assumes no data center compute sales to China at all.\"], \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\", \"period\": \"next quarter (quarter following the latest reported quarter)\", \"value\": \"$108 billion\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"company outlook, excluding any China compute sales\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}]}, {\"id\": \"e4\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA recently agreed to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.9 billion to expand its software capabilities, as reported by Fool.com; the item does not say the transaction has closed.\", \"passages\": [\"The company also recently agreed to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.9 billion to expand its software capabilities.\"], \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": \"recently\", \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"acquisition consideration for Hugging Face\", \"period\": null, \"value\": \"approximately $12.9 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"agreed acquisition, not stated as completed\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}]}, {\"id\": \"e5\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA disclosed that sales to two direct customers represented 22% and 14% of total revenue in fiscal 2026, respectively, a customer concentration the article flags as a risk.\", \"passages\": [\"Nvidia relies on a concentrated customer base, with sales to two direct customers representing 22% and 14% of total revenue in fiscal 2026, respectively.\"], \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"sales to largest direct customer as share of total revenue\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\", \"value\": \"22%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"direct customer, company total revenue\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"sales to second direct customer as share of total revenue\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\", \"value\": \"14%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"direct customer, company total revenue\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}]}]}, {\"answer\": \"B34\", \"article\": 4, \"statements_with_figures\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Wall Street consensus, as cited by Motley Fool's Jeremy Bowman, expects Nvidia to grow revenue by 91% this year and 66% next year (the article separately refers to fiscal 2028 as ending in January 2028, implying 'this year' is fiscal 2027 and 'next year' fiscal 2028).\", \"passages\": [\"Wall Street expects the company to grow revenue by 91% this year and 66% next year.\"], \"statement_party\": \"Wall Street analysts (consensus)\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"Revenue growth\", \"period\": \"this year\", \"value\": \"91%\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"Wall Street consensus expectations as reported by the article; year not explicitly labelled fiscal\", \"party\": \"Wall Street analysts (consensus)\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"Revenue growth\", \"period\": \"next year\", \"value\": \"66%\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"Wall Street consensus expectations as reported by the article; year not explicitly labelled fiscal\", \"party\": \"Wall Street analysts (consensus)\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"Consensus expectations, as reported by Motley Fool's Jeremy Bowman, put Nvidia's earnings per share for fiscal 2028 (the year ending January 2028) at $15.68.\", \"passages\": [\"For fiscal 2028, which ends in January of that year, Nvidia is expected to have earnings per share of $15.68.\"], \"statement_party\": \"Wall Street analysts (consensus)\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"Earnings per share\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2028 (year ending January 2028)\", \"value\": \"$15.68\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"Consensus expectation as reported by the article; GAAP/non-GAAP basis not stated\", \"party\": \"Wall Street analysts (consensus)\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}]}, {\"answer\": \"B35\", \"article\": 8, \"statements_with_figures\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia disclosed that sales to two direct customers represented 22% and 14% of its total revenue in fiscal 2026 (the year ended January 2026), respectively, a level of customer concentration the article flags as a risk to the business.\", \"passages\": [\"Nvidia relies on a concentrated customer base, with sales to two direct customers representing 22% and 14% of total revenue in fiscal 2026, respectively. This level of customer concentration adds a layer of risk to the business.\"], \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"sales to a direct customer as a share of total revenue\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\", \"value\": \"22%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"direct customer, share of total revenue\", \"party\": \"Nvidia\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"sales to a direct customer as a share of total revenue\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\", \"value\": \"14%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"direct customer, share of total revenue\", \"party\": \"Nvidia\"}]}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia recently agreed to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.9 billion to expand its software capabilities, according to the article, which does not describe the transaction as completed.\", \"passages\": [\"The company also recently agreed to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.9 billion to expand its software capabilities.\"], \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\", \"statement_date\": \"recently\", \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"acquisition consideration for Hugging Face\", \"period\": null, \"value\": \"approximately $12.9 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"agreed acquisition, not stated as completed\", \"party\": \"Nvidia\"}]}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia reported fiscal 2026 (year ended January 2026) revenue of nearly $215.9 billion, up roughly 65.5% year over year, net income of approximately $120.1 billion and a net margin of approximately 55.6%, versus a 55.8% net margin in the previous fiscal year.\", \"passages\": [\"Revenue growth remains a primary driver for the business. In fiscal 2026, which ended in January 2026, revenue reached nearly $215.9 billion, up roughly 65.5% year over year. This scale produced net income of approximately $120.1 billion. The net margin remained remarkably high at approximately 55.6%, consistent with the 55.8% net margin achieved in the previous fiscal year.\"], \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\", \"value\": \"nearly $215.9 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"Nvidia\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"year-over-year revenue growth\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\", \"value\": \"roughly 65.5%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"Nvidia\"}, {\"n\": 3, \"measure\": \"net income\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\", \"value\": \"approximately $120.1 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"Nvidia\"}, {\"n\": 4, \"measure\": \"net margin\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\", \"value\": \"approximately 55.6%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"Nvidia\"}, {\"n\": 5, \"measure\": \"net margin\", \"period\": \"previous fiscal year\", \"value\": \"55.8%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"Nvidia\"}]}, {\"id\": \"e4\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia's data center business brought in $89 billion in its latest quarter, and management guided the next quarter to $108 billion excluding any China compute sales; management's outlook for the current quarter assumes no data center compute sales to China at all.\", \"passages\": [\"Nvidia's data center business brought in $89 billion in its latest quarter alone, and management guided the next quarter to $108 billion, excluding any China compute sales.\", \"Revenue in its latest quarter also more than doubled, with almost all of it coming from data center customers, and management's outlook for the current quarter assumes no data center compute sales to China at all.\"], \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"data center revenue\", \"period\": \"latest quarter\", \"value\": \"$89 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"data center business\", \"party\": \"Nvidia\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\", \"period\": \"next quarter\", \"value\": \"$108 billion\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"excluding any China compute sales\", \"party\": \"Nvidia\"}]}]}, {\"answer\": \"B36\", \"article\": 8, \"statements_with_figures\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's data center business generated $89 billion of revenue in its latest reported quarter, and total revenue in that quarter more than doubled year over year, with almost all of it coming from data center customers, according to the article.\", \"passages\": [\"Nvidia's data center business brought in $89 billion in its latest quarter alone\", \"Revenue in its latest quarter also more than doubled, with almost all of it coming from data center customers\"], \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"data center revenue\", \"period\": \"latest quarter\", \"value\": \"$89 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"data center business\", \"party\": \"Nvidia\"}]}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's management guided revenue for the next quarter to $108 billion, an outlook that assumes no data center compute sales to China at all, according to the article.\", \"passages\": [\"management guided the next quarter to $108 billion, excluding any China compute sales\", \"management's outlook for the current quarter assumes no data center compute sales to China at all\"], \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\", \"period\": \"next quarter\", \"value\": \"$108 billion\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"excluding any China compute sales\", \"party\": \"Nvidia\"}]}, {\"id\": \"e4\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA disclosed that sales to two direct customers represented 22% and 14% of its total revenue, respectively, in fiscal 2026.\", \"passages\": [\"Nvidia relies on a concentrated customer base, with sales to two direct customers representing 22% and 14% of total revenue in fiscal 2026, respectively.\"], \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"share of total revenue from one direct customer\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\", \"value\": \"22%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"direct customer\", \"party\": \"Nvidia\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"share of total revenue from one direct customer\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\", \"value\": \"14%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"direct customer\", \"party\": \"Nvidia\"}]}]}, {\"answer\": \"B37\", \"article\": 1, \"statements_with_figures\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's latest reported quarter generated $89 billion of data-center revenue out of $96.2 billion in total revenue, meaning roughly 92.5% of sales came from the infrastructure businesses powering large-scale AI, according to GuruFocus's report on the company.\", \"passages\": [\"Nvidia's latest quarter generated $89 billion of data-center revenue out of $96.2 billion overall, meaning roughly 92.5% of sales came from the infrastructure businesses powering large-scale AI.\"], \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"data-center revenue\", \"period\": \"latest reported quarter\", \"value\": \"$89 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"data-center business\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"total revenue\", \"period\": \"latest reported quarter\", \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"company overall\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 3, \"measure\": \"share of sales from infrastructure businesses powering large-scale AI\", \"period\": \"latest reported quarter\", \"value\": \"roughly 92.5%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"derived by the article from data-center revenue versus total revenue\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}]}, {\"answer\": \"B38\", \"article\": 2, \"statements_with_figures\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia's revenue and net income are each expected to grow by roughly 90% or more in its current fiscal year, according to expectations cited by Quartz alongside the company's valuation discussion. The item does not name the parties producing the estimate.\", \"passages\": [\"even as the chipmaker's revenue and net income are each expected to grow by roughly 90% or more in its current fiscal year\"], \"statement_party\": null, \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"revenue growth\", \"period\": \"current fiscal year\", \"value\": \"roughly 90% or more\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"expected; growth rate stated as roughly 90% or more\", \"party\": null}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"net income growth\", \"period\": \"current fiscal year\", \"value\": \"roughly 90% or more\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"expected; growth rate stated as roughly 90% or more\", \"party\": null}], \"links\": \"none\"}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia's gross margin came in at 75% in the most recent quarter reported, Quartz said, citing the company's prior results.\", \"passages\": [\"Nvidia 's gross margin came in at 75% last quarter\"], \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"gross margin\", \"period\": \"last quarter\", \"value\": \"75%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"Analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg show Nvidia's gross margin sliding under 72% by the fourth quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter; rising costs for key components such as memory chips are cited as a central factor.\", \"passages\": [\"analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg show it sliding under 72% by the fourth quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter\", \"Rising costs for key components such as memory chips are a central factor.\"], \"statement_party\": \"analysts compiled by Bloomberg\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"gross margin\", \"period\": \"fourth quarter\", \"value\": \"under 72%\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg\", \"party\": \"analysts compiled by Bloomberg\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}, {\"id\": \"e4\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia's revenue more than doubled in its second quarter, according to Quartz's account of the company's quarterly results.\", \"passages\": [\"when revenue more than doubled\"], \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"period\": \"second quarter\", \"value\": \"more than doubled\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}, {\"id\": \"e5\", \"summary\": \"In its second-quarter earnings, Nvidia projected 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028, well above the 45% analysts had expected at the time, Quartz reported.\", \"passages\": [\"the company projected 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 — well above the 45% analysts had expected at the time\"], \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"revenue growth\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\", \"value\": \"70%\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"company projection\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"revenue growth expected by analysts\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\", \"value\": \"45%\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"analysts had expected at the time\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}]}, {\"answer\": \"B39\", \"article\": 7, \"statements_with_figures\": [{\"id\": \"ev1\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management, as reported by Trefis, expects Vera Rubin, its newest platform, to be the fastest product ramp in the company's history, and puts NVIDIA's revenue opportunity at about $40 billion per gigawatt of capacity with Vera Rubin versus $25 billion per gigawatt with Blackwell.\", \"passages\": [\"NVIDIA expects Vera Rubin, its newest platform, to be the fastest product ramp in the company’s history. Management puts NVIDIA’s revenue opportunity at about $40 billion per gigawatt of capacity with Vera Rubin, against $25 billion with Blackwell.\"], \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"revenue opportunity per gigawatt of capacity with Vera Rubin\", \"period\": null, \"value\": \"about $40 billion\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"per gigawatt of capacity, Vera Rubin platform\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"revenue opportunity per gigawatt of capacity with Blackwell\", \"period\": null, \"value\": \"$25 billion\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"per gigawatt of capacity, Blackwell platform\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": [{\"question\": \"clean-4\", \"new_question\": false, \"reason\": \"Management's per-gigawatt revenue opportunity for Vera Rubin ($40B) versus Blackwell ($25B) quantifies the value of the Vera Rubin ramp that clean-4 tracks; it is a management outlook, not a shipment confirmation.\"}]}, {\"id\": \"ev3\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management guided fiscal 2028 revenue growth of about 70%, the first time it has guided a full year ahead, as reported by Trefis.\", \"passages\": [\"For fiscal 2028, management has guided about 70% revenue growth, the first time it has guided a full year ahead.\"], \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"revenue growth\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\", \"value\": \"about 70%\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"company-wide revenue growth; first full-year-ahead guidance\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}, {\"id\": \"ev6\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs; management concedes some will call this circular financing and says the compute can be redeployed to other customers, as reported by Trefis.\", \"passages\": [\"Some of that demand also leans on NVIDIA’s own balance sheet. NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs. Management concedes some will call this circular financing and says the compute can be redeployed to other customers.\"], \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"investment in frontier AI labs\", \"period\": null, \"value\": \"nearly $50 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"equity investment; cumulative\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": [{\"question\": \"clean-1\", \"new_question\": false, \"reason\": \"Reports NVIDIA's roughly $50 billion of equity investment in frontier AI labs — the equity-investment side clean-1 compares against guarantees/credit support — and management's response to the 'circular financing' concern.\"}]}, {\"id\": \"ev7\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided its gross margin lower to settle in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, as memory prices have risen more than management expected, partly offset by price increases NVIDIA has already executed, as reported by Trefis.\", \"passages\": [\"Gross margin is guided lower. Memory prices have risen more than management expected, and the guide has that margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, helped by price increases NVIDIA has already executed.\"], \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"gross margin\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\", \"value\": \"72% to 73%\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"company gross margin; reflects higher memory costs partly offset by executed price increases\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}, {\"id\": \"ev8\", \"summary\": \"The 33 analyst estimates of NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 earnings per share range from $12.62 to $17.49 a share, a wide band for the year the forward multiple is priced on, according to Trefis.\", \"passages\": [\"Analysts also disagree on what that growth earns. The 33 estimates of fiscal 2028 earnings run from $12.62 to $17.49 a share, a wide band for the year the forward multiple is priced on.\"], \"statement_party\": \"Trefis\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"earnings per share\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\", \"value\": \"$12.62 to $17.49 a share\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"range across 33 analyst estimates\", \"party\": \"Trefis\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}]}, {\"answer\": \"B40\", \"article\": 10, \"statements_with_figures\": [{\"id\": \"ev-1\", \"summary\": \"On NVIDIA's fiscal Q2 2027 earnings call, management said inventory rose to $32 billion as the company prepared for the Vera Rubin launch.\", \"passages\": [\"Part of the cash has gone into inventory. On the fiscal Q2 2027 call, management said inventory rose to $32 billion as NVIDIA prepared for the Vera Rubin launch.\"], \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"inventory\", \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\", \"value\": \"$32 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": [{\"question\": \"clean-4\", \"new_question\": false, \"reason\": \"A $32 billion inventory build made in preparation for the Vera Rubin launch is a supply-side signal on how far the ramp toward volume shipments of Vera Rubin has progressed; it does not itself confirm shipments or revenue recognition.\"}]}, {\"id\": \"ev-2\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management guided fiscal Q3 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, while the consensus forecast for that quarter is $111.3 billion, above the top of that guidance range.\", \"passages\": [\"The price appears to assume more revenue than NVIDIA itself has forecast for fiscal Q3 2027. Management guided revenue for that quarter to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. The consensus forecast for that quarter is $111.3 billion, above the top of that range.\"], \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA (guidance); consensus estimate as reported by Trefis\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\", \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\", \"value\": \"$108 billion\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"plus or minus 2%\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA (guidance); consensus estimate as reported by Trefis\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"consensus revenue forecast\", \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\", \"value\": \"$111.3 billion\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"consensus forecast, above the top of NVIDIA's guidance range\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA (guidance); consensus estimate as reported by Trefis\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}, {\"id\": \"ev-3\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management said fiscal Q2 2027 revenue was $96 billion, more than double the year-earlier figure, with growth accelerating for the fourth consecutive quarter.\", \"passages\": [\"That expectation has a record behind it. Management said fiscal Q2 2027 revenue of $96 billion more than doubled from a year earlier. Growth also sped up for the fourth quarter in a row.\"], \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\", \"value\": \"$96 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"more than doubled from a year earlier; growth accelerated for the fourth consecutive quarter\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}, {\"id\": \"ev-4\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided gross margin for fiscal Q3 2027 to 74%, plus or minus half a point, down from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027, with management pointing to higher component costs, especially memory.\", \"passages\": [\"Gross margin, the share of revenue left after making the product, is a different measure from operating margin. For fiscal Q3 2027 it is guided to 74%, plus or minus half a point, down from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027. Management pointed to higher component costs, especially memory.\"], \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"gross margin guidance\", \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\", \"value\": \"74%\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"plus or minus half a point; attributed to higher component costs, especially memory\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"gross margin\", \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\", \"value\": \"75%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}, {\"id\": \"ev-5\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management expects the newly launched Vera Rubin platform to make up about 20% of its data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027.\", \"passages\": [\"The newly launched Vera Rubin platform is one source of that revenue. Management expects it to make up about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027.\"], \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"Vera Rubin share of data center revenue\", \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\", \"value\": \"about 20%\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"of data center revenue; management expectation\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": [{\"question\": \"clean-4\", \"new_question\": false, \"reason\": \"Management's expectation that Vera Rubin will already contribute about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027 bears on whether the platform reaches volume customer shipments and first revenue recognition within fiscal 2027; it is a forecast, not confirmed shipment or revenue.\"}]}, {\"id\": \"ev-7\", \"summary\": \"Trefis reports that NVIDIA's operating margin is 65%, its highest in ten years, against a three-year average of 59%.\", \"passages\": [\"But a low P/E only stays low if profit holds up. NVIDIA’s operating margin, the share of revenue left after running costs, is 65%, its highest in ten years. Its three-year average is 59%.\"], \"statement_party\": \"Trefis (analysis of NVIDIA reported figures)\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"operating margin\", \"period\": null, \"value\": \"65%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"highest in ten years\", \"party\": \"Trefis (analysis of NVIDIA reported figures)\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"operating margin, three-year average\", \"period\": \"trailing three years\", \"value\": \"59%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"three-year average\", \"party\": \"Trefis (analysis of NVIDIA reported figures)\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}]}]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/figure-fields-2/reader-packet-1-part4.json\\\")); [print(json.dumps(a,ensure_ascii=False,indent=1)) for a in p[\\\"answers\\\"] if a[\\\"answer\\\"] in (\\\"B32\\\",\\\"B33\\\",\\\"B34\\\",\\\"B35\\\")]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/figure-fields-2/reader-packet-1-part4.json\\\")); [print(json.dumps(a,ensure_ascii=False,indent=1)) for a in p[\\\"answers\\\"] if a[\\\"answer\\\"] in (\\\"B32\\\",\\\"B33\\\",\\\"B34\\\",\\\"B35\\\")]'\"","aggregated_output":"{\n \"answer\": \"B32\",\n \"article\": 7,\n \"statements_with_figures\": [\n  {\n   \"id\": \"e1\",\n   \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management said it expects Vera Rubin, its newest platform, to be the fastest product ramp in the company's history, and put NVIDIA's revenue opportunity at about $40 billion per gigawatt of capacity with Vera Rubin, against $25 billion with Blackwell.\",\n   \"passages\": [\n    \"NVIDIA expects Vera Rubin, its newest platform, to be the fastest product ramp in the company’s history. Management puts NVIDIA’s revenue opportunity at about $40 billion per gigawatt of capacity with Vera Rubin, against $25 billion with Blackwell.\"\n   ],\n   \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA management\",\n   \"statement_date\": null,\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"n\": 1,\n     \"measure\": \"revenue opportunity per gigawatt of capacity with Vera Rubin\",\n     \"period\": null,\n     \"value\": \"about $40 billion per gigawatt of capacity\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"revenue opportunity, Vera Rubin\",\n     \"party\": \"NVIDIA management\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"n\": 2,\n     \"measure\": \"revenue opportunity per gigawatt of capacity with Blackwell\",\n     \"period\": null,\n     \"value\": \"$25 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"as stated, per gigawatt of capacity with Blackwell\",\n     \"party\": \"NVIDIA management\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"links\": [\n    {\n     \"question\": \"clean-4\",\n     \"new_question\": false,\n     \"reason\": \"Management's per-gigawatt revenue opportunity for Vera Rubin (~$40B vs ~$25B for Blackwell) and its expectation of the fastest ramp in company history give the unit economics and expected scale of the Vera Rubin ramp tracked in clean-4; they carry no shipment date and do not show that customer shipments have occurred.\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"e3\",\n   \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management guided about 70% revenue growth for fiscal 2028, the first time it has guided a full year ahead, and said the guide does not depend on finding more demand; the CEO said demand is much greater than 70% and that supply is what holds the guide there.\",\n   \"passages\": [\n    \"For fiscal 2028, management has guided about 70% revenue growth, the first time it has guided a full year ahead. That guide does not depend on finding more demand. The CEO says demand is much greater than 70%, and that supply is what holds the guide there.\"\n   ],\n   \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA management (CEO)\",\n   \"statement_date\": null,\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"n\": 1,\n     \"measure\": \"revenue growth\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n     \"value\": \"about 70%\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"full-year guidance, first time the company has guided a full year ahead\",\n     \"party\": \"NVIDIA management\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"links\": [\n    {\n     \"question\": \"clean-4\",\n     \"new_question\": false,\n     \"reason\": \"A ~70% revenue growth guide for fiscal 2028 is management's forecast for the year the Vera Rubin ramp scales and so bears on the ramp's expected magnitude; it is a later-year revenue forecast and does not date FY2027 shipments or establish volumes.\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"e5\",\n   \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided gross margin lower, with memory prices having risen more than management expected; the guide has that margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, helped by price increases NVIDIA has already executed. The article notes consensus assumes margins hold roughly steady.\",\n   \"passages\": [\n    \"Consensus has earnings and revenue growing at a similar pace between fiscal 2027 and fiscal 2028, so analysts assume profit margins hold roughly steady. Gross margin is guided lower. Memory prices have risen more than management expected, and the guide has that margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, helped by price increases NVIDIA has already executed.\"\n   ],\n   \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA management\",\n   \"statement_date\": null,\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"n\": 1,\n     \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n     \"value\": \"72% to 73%\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"guided range, after memory-cost increases, helped by price increases already executed\",\n     \"party\": \"NVIDIA management\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"links\": \"none\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"e6\",\n   \"summary\": \"NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs; management concedes some will call this circular financing and says the compute can be redeployed to other customers.\",\n   \"passages\": [\n    \"Some of that demand also leans on NVIDIA’s own balance sheet. NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs. Management concedes some will call this circular financing and says the compute can be redeployed to other customers.\"\n   ],\n   \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n   \"statement_date\": null,\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"n\": 1,\n     \"measure\": \"investment in frontier AI labs\",\n     \"period\": null,\n     \"value\": \"nearly $50 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": null,\n     \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"links\": [\n    {\n     \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n     \"new_question\": false,\n     \"reason\": \"The nearly $50 billion NVIDIA has invested in frontier AI labs is the equity-investment side of clean-1's comparison against guarantees and credit support; management's answer to 'circular financing' criticism is its own statement, not a disclosure of guarantee amounts.\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"e7\",\n   \"summary\": \"The 33 analysts' estimates of NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 earnings range from $12.62 to $17.49 a share, which the article calls a wide band for the year the forward multiple is priced on.\",\n   \"passages\": [\n    \"Analysts also disagree on what that growth earns. The 33 estimates of fiscal 2028 earnings run from $12.62 to $17.49 a share, a wide band for the year the forward multiple is priced on.\"\n   ],\n   \"statement_party\": \"Analysts (33 estimates compiled in the article)\",\n   \"statement_date\": null,\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"n\": 1,\n     \"measure\": \"earnings per share estimate range across 33 estimates\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n     \"value\": \"$12.62 to $17.49 a share\",\n     \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n     \"basis\": \"33 estimates\",\n     \"party\": \"analysts\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"links\": \"none\"\n  }\n ]\n}\n{\n \"answer\": \"B33\",\n \"article\": 8,\n \"statements_with_figures\": [\n  {\n   \"id\": \"e1\",\n   \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's fiscal 2026 (year ended January 2026) revenue reached nearly $215.9 billion, up roughly 65.5% year over year, producing net income of approximately $120.1 billion and a net margin of approximately 55.6%, consistent with the 55.8% net margin of the prior fiscal year, according to Fool.com's comparison article.\",\n   \"passages\": [\n    \"In fiscal 2026, which ended in January 2026, revenue reached nearly $215.9 billion, up roughly 65.5% year over year. This scale produced net income of approximately $120.1 billion. The net margin remained remarkably high at approximately 55.6%, consistent with the 55.8% net margin achieved in the previous fiscal year.\"\n   ],\n   \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n   \"statement_date\": null,\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"n\": 1,\n     \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal 2026 (ended January 2026)\",\n     \"value\": \"nearly $215.9 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"company total revenue, fiscal year\",\n     \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"n\": 2,\n     \"measure\": \"revenue growth year over year\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\",\n     \"value\": \"roughly 65.5%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"company total revenue, fiscal year\",\n     \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"n\": 3,\n     \"measure\": \"net income\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\",\n     \"value\": \"approximately $120.1 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"company total, fiscal year\",\n     \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"n\": 4,\n     \"measure\": \"net margin\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\",\n     \"value\": \"approximately 55.6%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"company total; prior fiscal year 55.8%\",\n     \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"e2\",\n   \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's total revenue more than doubled year over year in its latest reported quarter, with almost all of it coming from data center customers, and its data center business generated $89 billion in that quarter alone, per Fool.com.\",\n   \"passages\": [\n    \"Revenue in its latest quarter also more than doubled, with almost all of it coming from data center customers, and management's outlook for the current quarter assumes no data center compute sales to China at all.\",\n    \"Nvidia's data center business brought in $89 billion in its latest quarter alone, and management guided the next quarter to $108 billion, excluding any China compute sales.\"\n   ],\n   \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n   \"statement_date\": null,\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"n\": 1,\n     \"measure\": \"data center revenue\",\n     \"period\": \"latest reported quarter (as of 25 September 2026 article)\",\n     \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"data center business\",\n     \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"e3\",\n   \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management guided its next quarter to $108 billion of revenue, with an outlook that assumes no data center compute sales to China at all (excluding any China compute sales), as reported by Fool.com.\",\n   \"passages\": [\n    \"Nvidia's data center business brought in $89 billion in its latest quarter alone, and management guided the next quarter to $108 billion, excluding any China compute sales.\",\n    \"Revenue in its latest quarter also more than doubled, with almost all of it coming from data center customers, and management's outlook for the current quarter assumes no data center compute sales to China at all.\"\n   ],\n   \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n   \"statement_date\": null,\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"n\": 1,\n     \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\",\n     \"period\": \"next quarter (quarter following the latest reported quarter)\",\n     \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"company outlook, excluding any China compute sales\",\n     \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"e4\",\n   \"summary\": \"NVIDIA recently agreed to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.9 billion to expand its software capabilities, as reported by Fool.com; the item does not say the transaction has closed.\",\n   \"passages\": [\n    \"The company also recently agreed to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.9 billion to expand its software capabilities.\"\n   ],\n   \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n   \"statement_date\": \"recently\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"n\": 1,\n     \"measure\": \"acquisition consideration for Hugging Face\",\n     \"period\": null,\n     \"value\": \"approximately $12.9 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"agreed acquisition, not stated as completed\",\n     \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"e5\",\n   \"summary\": \"NVIDIA disclosed that sales to two direct customers represented 22% and 14% of total revenue in fiscal 2026, respectively, a customer concentration the article flags as a risk.\",\n   \"passages\": [\n    \"Nvidia relies on a concentrated customer base, with sales to two direct customers representing 22% and 14% of total revenue in fiscal 2026, respectively.\"\n   ],\n   \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n   \"statement_date\": null,\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"n\": 1,\n     \"measure\": \"sales to largest direct customer as share of total revenue\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\",\n     \"value\": \"22%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"direct customer, company total revenue\",\n     \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"n\": 2,\n     \"measure\": \"sales to second direct customer as share of total revenue\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\",\n     \"value\": \"14%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"direct customer, company total revenue\",\n     \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n    }\n   ]\n  }\n ]\n}\n{\n \"answer\": \"B34\",\n \"article\": 4,\n \"statements_with_figures\": [\n  {\n   \"id\": \"e1\",\n   \"summary\": \"Wall Street consensus, as cited by Motley Fool's Jeremy Bowman, expects Nvidia to grow revenue by 91% this year and 66% next year (the article separately refers to fiscal 2028 as ending in January 2028, implying 'this year' is fiscal 2027 and 'next year' fiscal 2028).\",\n   \"passages\": [\n    \"Wall Street expects the company to grow revenue by 91% this year and 66% next year.\"\n   ],\n   \"statement_party\": \"Wall Street analysts (consensus)\",\n   \"statement_date\": null,\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"n\": 1,\n     \"measure\": \"Revenue growth\",\n     \"period\": \"this year\",\n     \"value\": \"91%\",\n     \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n     \"basis\": \"Wall Street consensus expectations as reported by the article; year not explicitly labelled fiscal\",\n     \"party\": \"Wall Street analysts (consensus)\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"n\": 2,\n     \"measure\": \"Revenue growth\",\n     \"period\": \"next year\",\n     \"value\": \"66%\",\n     \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n     \"basis\": \"Wall Street consensus expectations as reported by the article; year not explicitly labelled fiscal\",\n     \"party\": \"Wall Street analysts (consensus)\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"links\": \"none\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"e2\",\n   \"summary\": \"Consensus expectations, as reported by Motley Fool's Jeremy Bowman, put Nvidia's earnings per share for fiscal 2028 (the year ending January 2028) at $15.68.\",\n   \"passages\": [\n    \"For fiscal 2028, which ends in January of that year, Nvidia is expected to have earnings per share of $15.68.\"\n   ],\n   \"statement_party\": \"Wall Street analysts (consensus)\",\n   \"statement_date\": null,\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"n\": 1,\n     \"measure\": \"Earnings per share\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal 2028 (year ending January 2028)\",\n     \"value\": \"$15.68\",\n     \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n     \"basis\": \"Consensus expectation as reported by the article; GAAP/non-GAAP basis not stated\",\n     \"party\": \"Wall Street analysts (consensus)\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"links\": \"none\"\n  }\n ]\n}\n{\n \"answer\": \"B35\",\n \"article\": 8,\n \"statements_with_figures\": [\n  {\n   \"id\": \"e1\",\n   \"summary\": \"Nvidia disclosed that sales to two direct customers represented 22% and 14% of its total revenue in fiscal 2026 (the year ended January 2026), respectively, a level of customer concentration the article flags as a risk to the business.\",\n   \"passages\": [\n    \"Nvidia relies on a concentrated customer base, with sales to two direct customers representing 22% and 14% of total revenue in fiscal 2026, respectively. This level of customer concentration adds a layer of risk to the business.\"\n   ],\n   \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n   \"statement_date\": null,\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"n\": 1,\n     \"measure\": \"sales to a direct customer as a share of total revenue\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\",\n     \"value\": \"22%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"direct customer, share of total revenue\",\n     \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"n\": 2,\n     \"measure\": \"sales to a direct customer as a share of total revenue\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\",\n     \"value\": \"14%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"direct customer, share of total revenue\",\n     \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"e2\",\n   \"summary\": \"Nvidia recently agreed to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.9 billion to expand its software capabilities, according to the article, which does not describe the transaction as completed.\",\n   \"passages\": [\n    \"The company also recently agreed to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.9 billion to expand its software capabilities.\"\n   ],\n   \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n   \"statement_date\": \"recently\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"n\": 1,\n     \"measure\": \"acquisition consideration for Hugging Face\",\n     \"period\": null,\n     \"value\": \"approximately $12.9 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"agreed acquisition, not stated as completed\",\n     \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"e3\",\n   \"summary\": \"Nvidia reported fiscal 2026 (year ended January 2026) revenue of nearly $215.9 billion, up roughly 65.5% year over year, net income of approximately $120.1 billion and a net margin of approximately 55.6%, versus a 55.8% net margin in the previous fiscal year.\",\n   \"passages\": [\n    \"Revenue growth remains a primary driver for the business. In fiscal 2026, which ended in January 2026, revenue reached nearly $215.9 billion, up roughly 65.5% year over year. This scale produced net income of approximately $120.1 billion. The net margin remained remarkably high at approximately 55.6%, consistent with the 55.8% net margin achieved in the previous fiscal year.\"\n   ],\n   \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n   \"statement_date\": null,\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"n\": 1,\n     \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\",\n     \"value\": \"nearly $215.9 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": null,\n     \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"n\": 2,\n     \"measure\": \"year-over-year revenue growth\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\",\n     \"value\": \"roughly 65.5%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": null,\n     \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"n\": 3,\n     \"measure\": \"net income\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\",\n     \"value\": \"approximately $120.1 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": null,\n     \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"n\": 4,\n     \"measure\": \"net margin\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\",\n     \"value\": \"approximately 55.6%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": null,\n     \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"n\": 5,\n     \"measure\": \"net margin\",\n     \"period\": \"previous fiscal year\",\n     \"value\": \"55.8%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": null,\n     \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"e4\",\n   \"summary\": \"Nvidia's data center business brought in $89 billion in its latest quarter, and management guided the next quarter to $108 billion excluding any China compute sales; management's outlook for the current quarter assumes no data center compute sales to China at all.\",\n   \"passages\": [\n    \"Nvidia's data center business brought in $89 billion in its latest quarter alone, and management guided the next quarter to $108 billion, excluding any China compute sales.\",\n    \"Revenue in its latest quarter also more than doubled, with almost all of it coming from data center customers, and management's outlook for the current quarter assumes no data center compute sales to China at all.\"\n   ],\n   \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n   \"statement_date\": null,\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"n\": 1,\n     \"measure\": \"data center revenue\",\n     \"period\": \"latest quarter\",\n     \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"data center business\",\n     \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"n\": 2,\n     \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\",\n     \"period\": \"next quarter\",\n     \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"excluding any China compute sales\",\n     \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n    }\n   ]\n  }\n ]\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"answer\":\"B31\",\"statements\":[{\"id\":\"ev-1\",\"statement_party\":\"right\",\"statement_date\":\"unknown_ok\",\"figures\":[{\"n\":1,\"party\":\"right\",\"measure\":\"right\",\"value\":\"right\",\"kind\":\"right\"},{\"n\":2,\"party\":\"right\",\"measure\":\"right\",\"value\":\"right\",\"kind\":\"right\"},{\"n\":3,\"party\":\"right\",\"measure\":\"right\",\"value\":\"right\",\"kind\":\"right\"}],\"links\":[],\"should_link\":null}],\"named\":[{\"figure\":1,\"statement\":\"ev-1\",\"n\":3}]}\n{\"answer\":\"B32\",\"statements\":[{\"id\":\"e1\",\"statement_party\":\"right\",\"statement_date\":\"unknown_ok\",\"figures\":[{\"n\":1,\"party\":\"right\",\"measure\":\"right\",\"value\":\"right\",\"kind\":\"right\"},{\"n\":2,\"party\":\"right\",\"measure\":\"right\",\"value\":\"right\",\"kind\":\"right\"}],\"links\":[{\"question\":\"clean-4\",\"mark\":\"supported\"}],\"should_link\":null},{\"id\":\"e3\",\"statement_party\":\"right\",\"statement_date\":\"unknown_ok\",\"figures\":[{\"n\":1,\"party\":\"right\",\"measure\":\"right\",\"value\":\"right\",\"kind\":\"right\"}],\"links\":[{\"question\":\"clean-4\",\"mark\":\"unsupported\"}],\"should_link\":null},{\"id\":\"e5\",\"statement_party\":\"right\",\"statement_date\":\"unknown_ok\",\"figures\":[{\"n\":1,\"party\":\"right\",\"measure\":\"right\",\"value\":\"right\",\"kind\":\"right\"}],\"links\":[],\"should_link\":null},{\"id\":\"e6\",\"statement_party\":\"right\",\"statement_date\":\"unknown_ok\",\"figures\":[{\"n\":1,\"party\":\"right\",\"measure\":\"right\",\"value\":\"right\",\"kind\":\"right\"}],\"links\":[{\"question\":\"clean-1\",\"mark\":\"unsupported\"}],\"should_link\":null},{\"id\":\"e7\",\"statement_party\":\"right\",\"statement_date\":\"unknown_ok\",\"figures\":[{\"n\":1,\"party\":\"right\",\"measure\":\"right\",\"value\":\"right\",\"kind\":\"right\"}],\"links\":[],\"should_link\":null}],\"named\":[{\"figure\":1,\"statement\":\"e3\",\"